/// What a block on a landing page is.
///
/// A closed list, and it closed before the first row was written. The sixth kind is a value here,
/// a shape in the zod union, a component with a story and a test, and a panel of settings — the
/// build breaks at every point that is missing, which is the reason this is an enum and not a
/// string.
///
/// It exists because everything the shopkeeper asked to arrange turned out to be one sentence: a
/// block, in an order, with settings. Before it there were three ways to order this page —
/// `StoreBanner.position`, `Store.layoutType`, and a `layoutSettings` blob with no order at all —
/// and putting a poster under the products needed a boolean of its own.
enum SectionKind {
  /// The cover at the top. One image, or several, which is what makes it a carousel.
  COVER
  /// A poster in the body of the page.
  BANNER
  /// A heading and a line under it. A sign, not a card.
  TEXT
  /// The band of promises: an icon, a title and a line, per row.
  BENEFITS
  /// The product rails. One per shop, undeletable, and a row like any other so it has a position —
  /// which is the whole reason `belowProducts` no longer exists.
  PRODUCTS
}

/// Edge to edge, or inside the page's measure. Only the cover ever bled to the edges; every other
/// band on the shop window already sits inside the page's own measure.
enum SectionWidth {
  FULL
  CONTAINED
}

/// Where a block sends the visitor.
///
/// This is the piece the first attempt lacked, and the reason it was removed. The old
/// `store_showcases` kept its destination as a free-text `href`, so a banner pointing at
/// `/lessari/blusas` became a dead link the day that category was renamed, and nothing said so.
/// Commit `5639c47` deleted the table and wrote down what went with it — "banner apontando para um
/// produto específico ou para fora da loja" — and this enum is that sentence being paid back.
///
/// CATEGORY and PRODUCT carry a foreign key, never an address: the address is built where it is
/// rendered, from the slug the row has now, so renaming the thing a block points at moves the block
/// with it. EXTERNAL is the only one that stores a string, because there is nothing here to point
/// at — another website, or a wa.me link.
enum SectionTarget {
  CATEGORY
  PRODUCT
  EXTERNAL
  /// A block that says something and goes nowhere — a season, a promise about delivery, a picture.
  /// The window draws it without an arrow and without making it clickable: a card that looks
  /// interactive and is not is worse than one that never offered.
  NONE
}

/// One block of a shop's landing page.
///
/// It is the `store_banners` table generalised, one day after that table shipped, and the rename
/// is the point rather than a tidy-up: a table called banners that holds a heading, a band of
/// promises and the product rails is a name that lies, and the next person adds a second table
/// beside it. This repository already learned that — `5639c47` deleted `store_showcases` with the
/// note that "dois nomes para uma coisa são duas telas para manter em dia".
///
/// Every block carries its own `position` rather than leaving the order to a layout document. That
/// is what lets a block appear the moment it is created, instead of waiting for an editor, and it
/// is why design mode is a writer of `position` rather than a prerequisite for seeing anything.
model StoreSection {
  id      String @id @default(uuid(7)) @db.Uuid
  storeId String @db.Uuid
  store   Store  @relation(fields: [storeId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind SectionKind

  /// Optional on every kind now, unlike a banner's. A cover is often a photograph with the shop's
  /// own words already in it, and a PRODUCTS row with no title uses the platform's word for the
  /// catalogue — which is what lets the shopkeeper rename "Todos os produtos" by typing, and undo
  /// it by clearing the field rather than by remembering what it used to say.
  title    String? @db.VarChar(120)
  /// The line under the title.
  subtitle String? @db.VarChar(200)

  /// Nullable, unlike a banner's. TEXT, BENEFITS and PRODUCTS draw no picture of their own — the
  /// rule that a BANNER must have one moved to the zod union, where it can be stated per kind
  /// instead of for every row in the table.
  imageUrl String?

  /// Full width, two across, three across. The same three shapes a category poster had, and the
  /// same enum: a second one would be two vocabularies for one row of the page.
  layout ShowcaseLayout @default(FULL)

  /// Read only on COVER. Every other band is already contained.
  width SectionWidth @default(FULL)

  target SectionTarget @default(NONE)

  /// Cascade, not SetNull, and the reason is the CHECK in the migration. A block whose target is a
  /// category must have that category: `SetNull` would leave a row whose `target` says CATEGORY
  /// with nowhere to go, which is the exact state the constraint exists to make impossible. The
  /// cost is real and worth naming — deleting a category takes its poster with it — and it is the
  /// same answer this schema already gives for a subcategory outliving its parent.
  categoryId String?          @db.Uuid
  category   ProductCategory? @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  productId String?  @db.Uuid
  product   Product? @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// `http` or `https` only, bounded by SECTION_URL_MAX_LENGTH. It is echoed verbatim into an
  /// anchor on the anonymous, indexed shop window, which is why the scheme is an allow-list rather
  /// than a deny-list — the same rule `store-fields.dto.ts` states for every other stored URL.
  externalUrl String?

  /// A COVER's slides, or a BENEFITS band's rows. Empty on every other kind.
  ///
  /// Json, and the difference from `layoutSettings` is the one that matters: this is **content**.
  /// Sixteen of that blob's twenty-one keys survived years unread because a key nobody draws is
  /// invisible; an `items` nobody draws is a blank band on the shop's front page, reported the
  /// same day. Its shape is decided by `kind` and enforced by a discriminated union in zod.
  items Json @default("[]")

  /// The order the shopkeeper chose. Ties break on title so the page is never arbitrary.
  position Int @default(0)

  /// Hiding a block keeps it. It is not a delete, and the picture is not re-uploaded to undo it.
  isActive Boolean @default(true)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// The storefront's read, in one index scan.
  @@index([storeId, isActive, position])
  @@map("store_sections")
}
